PLACES=[
N('23rd-regiment-armory-brooklyn','23rd Regiment Armory (Bedford Atlantic Armory)','Brooklyn (Crown Heights)','nyc','fort','standing',40.67823,-73.95303,'exact','view','drive','','1891–1895',
  'A castle-like National Guard armory at 1322 Bedford Avenue, between Atlantic Avenue and Pacific Street, built in 1891–1895 for the 23rd Regiment of the New York State Militia. Fowler & Hough of Brooklyn designed it in the Romanesque Revival style with Isaac Perry, the state architect; the drill room was not finished until 1902. The city turned it into the Bedford-Atlantic men\'s homeless shelter in 1982, and it is still one. It is a New York City landmark (1977) and on the National Register (1980).',
  'The Bedford Avenue front: deep red pressed brick, brownish-red Potsdam stone and red terracotta, with towers and battlements, and the long drill hall behind with ten windowless bays between brick pilasters. Interior not checked; it is not open to visitors.',
  'Brooklyn built itself a red castle, and for forty years it has been the city\'s largest men\'s shelter.',
  'City-run homeless shelter and intake centre, with a police training facility inside (Wikipedia): no public entry, and people live here, so keep cameras on the building. '+ST,
  [W('23rd Regiment Armory')],brick='yes'),
N('13th-regiment-armory-brooklyn','13th Regiment Armory (Sumner Avenue Armory)','Brooklyn (Bedford–Stuyvesant)','nyc','fort','standing',40.68492,-73.93828,'exact','view','drive','','1892–1894',
  'The armory of the 13th Regiment, designed by Rudolph L. Daus and built in 1892–1894 at 357 Marcus Garvey Boulevard (once Sumner Avenue), between Putnam and Jefferson Avenues. Its cost rose from a $300,000 award to nearly $700,000, which the Brooklyn Daily Eagle called a scandal. A fire in 1944 burned many of the regiment\'s trophies. Since 1987 or 1988 it has been a men\'s homeless shelter, now called Pamoja House.',
  'The Marcus Garvey Boulevard front: a great round-arched sally port between two round towers, each 28 feet across; the south tower has an observatory and the north one a smaller turret on top as a lookout. Interior not checked; it is not open to visitors.',
  '',
  'A working men\'s shelter under the city\'s Department of Homeless Services (Wikipedia): no public entry, and people live here. '+ST,
  [W('13th Regiment Armory')],brick='yes'),
N('14th-regiment-armory-brooklyn','14th Regiment Armory (Park Slope Armory)','Brooklyn (South Slope)','nyc','fort','standing',40.66255,-73.98222,'exact','view','drive','','1891–1895',
  'A brick and stone castle of an armory on Eighth Avenue between 14th and 15th Streets, built in 1891–95 to William A. Mundell\'s design for the 14th Regiment, the "Fighting Fourteenth" of the Civil War. Part of it became a women\'s homeless shelter in 1986, and a sports complex run by the Prospect Park YMCA opened in the drill hall in 2010. It was listed on the National Register in 1994 and made a city landmark in 1998.',
  'The Eighth Avenue front: brick on a foundation of Warsaw bluestone, a four-storey tower with a rounded bartizan, a three-storey tower and the sally port between them, under brick cornices with machicolations. Wikipedia says a veterans\' museum is inside; its hours were not checked. A Tiffany stained-glass window from the main stair was moved to Staten Island in the late 1990s.',
  '',
  'State armory building shared by a women\'s shelter, the Prospect Park YMCA\'s sports complex and a veterans\' museum (Wikipedia). The YMCA and museum hours were not checked, so treat it as a building to look at from Eighth Avenue. '+ST,
  [W('14th Regiment Armory')],brick='yes'),
N('alhambra-apartments-brooklyn','Alhambra Apartments','Brooklyn (Bedford–Stuyvesant)','nyc','landmark','standing',40.68158,-73.94997,'exact','view','drive','','1889–1890',
  'An apartment house at 500–518 Nostrand Avenue, designed by Montrose Morris and built by the developer Louis F. Seitz in 1889–90. It is an early American example of a multiple-family building planned with large flats for well-off tenants. The city made it a landmark in 1986. Girls\' High School stands across the avenue.',
  'Six towers, loggias, gables, arcades and a great deal of terracotta ornament along the Nostrand Avenue front. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private apartments: view only. '+ST,
  [W('Alhambra Apartments')],brick='yes'),
N('brooklyn-borough-hall','Brooklyn Borough Hall (Brooklyn City Hall)','Brooklyn (Downtown)','nyc','civic','standing',40.69264,-73.99054,'exact','public','drive','','1836–1848',
  'The city hall of the independent City of Brooklyn, in Greek Revival style and Tuckahoe marble. Calvin Pollard won the design contest and the cornerstone was laid in 1836, but money ran out; work began again in 1845 to a design by Gamaliel King made to fit Pollard\'s foundations, and the building was finished in 1848. A fire on February 26, 1895 destroyed the cupola, its statue of Justice and the top floors, and a new cast-iron cupola went up three years later. When Brooklyn joined New York City in 1898 it became Borough Hall. It has been a city landmark since 1966.',
  'The marble front and its columned portico facing the plaza, and the cast-iron cupola of 1898 with a figure of Justice. A Beaux-Arts courtroom of 1902 by Axel Hedman is inside; whether visitors may go in was not checked.',
  '',
  'City of New York; the seat of the Brooklyn Borough President at 209 Joralemon Street. The plaza in front is public and connects by a mall to Cadman Plaza Park. Interior access not checked. Street parking is scarce downtown; no lot was looked for.',
  [W('Brooklyn Borough Hall')],brick='no'),
N('boys-high-school-brooklyn','Boys High School','Brooklyn (Bedford–Stuyvesant)','nyc','civic','standing',40.68472,-73.94727,'exact','view','drive','','1891',
  'A Romanesque Revival school put up in 1891 on the west side of Marcy Avenue between Putnam Avenue and Madison Street, designed by James W. Naughton, Superintendent of Buildings for Brooklyn\'s Board of Education, and counted his finest work. The city landmarked it in 1975, the same year the school merged with Girls\' High School and left for a new site; it went on the National Register in 1982. Its outside played the Knickerbocker Hospital in the television series The Knick.',
  'The round corner tower, the dormers, the tall campanile and terracotta ornament somewhat in the manner of Louis Sullivan. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A public school building (present use not checked): view from Marcy Avenue. '+ST,
  [W('Boys High School (Brooklyn)')],brick='yes'),
N('girls-high-school-brooklyn','Girls\' High School','Brooklyn (Bedford–Stuyvesant)','nyc','civic','standing',40.68130,-73.94961,'exact','view','drive','','1886',
  'Brooklyn\'s public high school grew out of the Central Grammar School of 1878. This building at 475 Nostrand Avenue was finished in 1886 and meant for boys and girls in separate wings, but by then there were so many students that only the girls moved in; the boys got their own building on Marcy Avenue in 1892. James W. Naughton designed it. The school merged with Boys High in 1975 and moved away.',
  'A Victorian Gothic front with pointed-arch windows under a French mansard roof, in two pavilions either side of a central entrance. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A public school building (present use not checked): view from Nostrand Avenue. '+ST,
  [W('Girls\' High School (Brooklyn)')],brick='yes'),
N('soldiers-sailors-arch-brooklyn','Soldiers\' and Sailors\' Arch, Grand Army Plaza','Brooklyn (Prospect Heights)','nyc','landmark','standing',40.673,-73.9699,'exact','public','walk','','1889–1892',
  'Brooklyn\'s memorial to the Union soldiers and sailors of the Civil War: a granite triumphal arch 80 feet tall, designed by John H. Duncan. The cornerstone was laid on October 30, 1889 and the arch was dedicated on October 21, 1892; the sculpture came afterwards. The Prospect Park Alliance finished an $8.9 million restoration in June 2025, the first major one in nearly fifty years.',
  'Frederick MacMonnies\'s bronze quadriga with Columbia on top and his Army and Navy groups on the south piers; inside the archway, under a coffered vault, bronze reliefs of Lincoln and Grant on horseback by Thomas Eakins and William O\'Donovan, dated 1893–1894 and installed in 1895. Spiral stairs inside lead to a room and an observation deck: the Alliance\'s page tells only of ranger tours by lottery, so do not count on going up.',
  '',
  'City parkland (NYC Parks), looked after with the Prospect Park Alliance; the arch stands in the middle of Grand Army Plaza at the north entrance to Prospect Park, and you walk up to it. The plaza is ringed by traffic: use the crossings. Regular hours for the inside are not given on the Alliance page read (prospectpark.org, June 2025 article). Parking: street parking; the nearest lot OpenStreetMap maps on public land is the Brooklyn Museum\'s on Washington Avenue, about 500 m southeast, not checked.',
  [W('Soldiers\' and Sailors\' Arch'),L('Prospect Park Alliance: Grand Army Plaza Arch is Restored','https://www.prospectpark.org/grand-army-plaza-arch-is-restored/')],brick='no'),
N('old-brooklyn-fire-headquarters','Old Brooklyn Fire Headquarters','Brooklyn (Downtown)','nyc','civic','standing',40.69272,-73.98696,'exact','view','drive','','1892',
  'The headquarters of the Brooklyn Fire Department, at 365–367 Jay Street near Willoughby Street, designed by Frank Freeman in the Richardsonian Romanesque style and built in 1892. Fire companies used it until the 1970s; it was later converted to apartments. A New York City landmark since 1966 and on the National Register since 1972.',
  'A five-storey front only 50 feet wide beside a seven-storey watchtower 126 feet tall. The street level is red Jonesboro granite trimmed with red Lake Superior sandstone, with a 16-foot arch where the engines came out; above is buff brick with terracotta trim, and turrets with terracotta finials. Interior not checked.',
  'A fire station built like a small castle, squeezed between the office blocks of MetroTech.',
  'Apartments: view only. '+ST,
  [W('Old Brooklyn Fire Headquarters')],brick='no'),
N('hotel-bossert-brooklyn','Hotel Bossert','Brooklyn (Brooklyn Heights)','nyc','hotel','standing',40.69507,-73.99571,'exact','view','drive','','1909',
  'The lumber magnate Louis Bossert built this 14-storey apartment hotel at 98 Montague Street in 1909, to an Italian Renaissance Revival design by Helmle & Huberty. Its two-level Marine Roof restaurant was a draw in the 1920s, and the Brooklyn Dodgers\' only World Series win was celebrated here. The Jehovah\'s Witnesses bought it in 1988 and used it until 2012. It has stood empty since while plans to reopen it stalled; SomeraRoad bought it in May 2025 to make apartments, work began in July 2026 and is planned to finish in 2028.',
  'The Montague Street front. The lobby\'s marble was largely replaced in the Witnesses\' restoration (Wikipedia). Closed: expect a building site.',
  '',
  'Private, vacant and under conversion to apartments as of 2026 (Wikipedia): view only. '+ST,
  [W('Hotel Bossert')],brick='no'),
N('montauk-club-brooklyn','Montauk Club','Brooklyn (Park Slope)','nyc','landmark','standing',40.67485,-73.97172,'exact','view','drive','','1889–1891',
  'A social club founded in 1889 for the fast-growing Park Slope, and the only one of Brooklyn\'s hundreds of clubs of that time still going. Francis H. Kimball designed the clubhouse, built in 1889–91, in Venetian Gothic, reportedly after the Ca\' d\'Oro on the Grand Canal. The upper and basement floors were later sold for condominiums; the club keeps the first and second floors.',
  'Brownstone and brick with verdigris copper and terracotta from the New York Architectural Terra Cotta Works. Look for the Montauk people in the capitals, over the main door and in a frieze running between the third and fourth floors, and for panels showing a 1659 meeting of Montauks and Europeans and the laying of the cornerstone. The smaller stair left of the main door was the ladies\' entrance and now serves the condominiums. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A private members\' club with condominiums above, at 25 Eighth Avenue: view from the street. Whether non-members may dine or visit was not checked. '+ST,
  [W('Montauk Club')],brick='some'),
N('queen-of-all-saints-brooklyn','Queen of All Saints Church','Brooklyn (Fort Greene)','nyc','worship','standing',40.68828,-73.96911,'exact','open','drive','','1910–1913',
  'A Catholic parish church at 300 Vanderbilt Avenue, on the corner of Lafayette Avenue, built with its school in 1910–13 to a Gothic Revival design by Reiley & Steinback and dedicated on Thanksgiving Day, November 27, 1913. The apse and rectory were finished in 1915. The parish dates from 1879. A spire stood over the Vanderbilt Avenue wing until the 1940s, and the school closed in 2019.',
  'The Gothic front and its long ranges of windows, without the spire old pictures show. Inside is the organ built in 1913 by Philipp Wirsching of Salem, Ohio, electrified in 1925 and cited by the Organ Historical Society. Glass and woodwork not checked.',
  '',
  'Roman Catholic Diocese of Brooklyn; an active parish, so come for a Mass or ask at the office (the parish site gives office hours Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.). Mass times from the parish\'s own page, qasrcc.org, read November 2026. '+ST,
  [W('Queen of All Saints Church (Brooklyn)'),L('Queen of All Saints parish: Mass times','https://www.qasrcc.org/mass-times')],brick='no',
  hours='Mass Saturday 5 p.m.; Sunday 8:30 and 10:30 a.m.; Monday to Friday 8:30 a.m.'),
N('eagle-warehouse-brooklyn','Eagle Warehouse & Storage Company','Brooklyn (Fulton Ferry)','nyc','industry','standing',40.70227,-73.99367,'exact','view','drive','','1894',
  'A fireproof furniture and silverware warehouse by the Brooklyn architect Frank Freeman, finished in 1894 for $300,000 on the site the Brooklyn Eagle newspaper had left, and built around the paper\'s old pressroom. Walt Whitman edited the Eagle here from 1846 to 1848. The warehouse was made into condominiums in 1980, when its middle was cut out for an atrium. It is part of the Fulton Ferry District, on the National Register since 1974 and a city landmark district since 1977.',
  'A brick fortress on Old Fulton Street: a bold Roman arch with the company\'s name in bronze letters leading into a barrel vault, small ground-floor windows behind iron grilles, and a battlemented parapet with the name again and a large clock, whose face is a penthouse window.',
  '',
  'Private condominiums at 28 Old Fulton Street: view only. '+ST,
  [W('Eagle Warehouse & Storage Company')],brick='yes'),
N('erasmus-hall-academy-brooklyn','Erasmus Hall Academy and High School','Brooklyn (Flatbush)','nyc','civic','standing',40.64951,-73.95826,'site','view','drive','','1787',
  'Erasmus Hall Academy was founded in 1786 on land given by the Flatbush Reformed Dutch Church, the first secondary school chartered by the New York State Regents, and its wooden building opened in 1787 with 26 students. It passed to the public school system in 1896. From 1904 C. B. J. Snyder built a Collegiate Gothic high school round a quadrangle with the old academy left standing in the middle. The city closed the high school in 1994 and the buildings now hold five small schools as the Erasmus Hall Educational Campus.',
  'From Flatbush Avenue: Snyder\'s buff-brick range with limestone and terracotta trim and a central tower with an oriel window. The 1787 academy, two and a half storeys of clapboard under a hipped roof, stands in the courtyard behind; Wikipedia says it holds a museum of the school\'s history. Whether it can be seen from the street, or visited, was not checked.',
  'A wooden schoolhouse of 1787 hidden inside a Gothic high school.',
  'New York City public school campus at 899–925 Flatbush Avenue: a working school, so view from the street only unless the school invites you. '+ST,
  [W('Erasmus Hall High School')],brick='no'),
N('st-ann-holy-trinity-brooklyn','St. Ann & the Holy Trinity Church','Brooklyn (Brooklyn Heights)','nyc','worship','standing',40.69463,-73.99280,'exact','view','drive','','1847',
  'Built as the Church of the Holy Trinity to Minard Lafever\'s design and opened in 1847, on the corner of Montague and Clinton Streets. The parish was closed in 1957 after years of dispute and the church stood mostly empty until 1969, when the congregation of St. Ann\'s, Brooklyn\'s oldest Episcopal parish, moved in. It was named a National Historic Landmark in 1987 and is the co-cathedral of the Episcopal Diocese of Long Island.',
  'The stained glass by William Jay Bolton and John Bolton: the first complete set of figural windows made in North America, 54 of the original 55 still in place (one from the organ loft is in the Metropolitan Museum). Also an Ernest Skinner organ, the third since 1847. The spire seen in old pictures is gone. The windows are seen from inside: service and visiting times not checked.',
  'America\'s first full set of figural stained glass, in a church that sat locked for twelve years.',
  'Episcopal Diocese of Long Island; an active church at 157 Montague Street. Its own site (stannholytrinity.org) refused to open in this session, so hours not checked. '+ST,
  [W('St. Ann & the Holy Trinity Church'),L('St. Ann & the Holy Trinity (parish site; not read)','https://stannholytrinity.org/')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('crescent-athletic-club-brooklyn','Crescent Athletic Club House (Saint Ann\'s School)','Brooklyn (Brooklyn Heights)','nyc','landmark','standing',40.69526,-73.99220,'exact','view','drive','','1906',
  'The clubhouse of the Crescent Athletic Club, one of New York\'s most successful sporting clubs, organized in 1884 and 1,500 strong by 1902. Frank Freeman designed it and it was finished in 1906 at 129 Pierrepont Street, on the corner of Clinton Street. The club went bankrupt in 1939 and left the next year; after years as offices, shops and a bowling alley the building was bought in 1966 for a school, and is now the Bosworth Building of Saint Ann\'s School. The front was renovated in 2000.',
  'A base of channelled limestone, light-coloured brick above with terracotta ornament, a terracotta frieze round the attic windows and a heavy cornice. Inside, murals survive in the hall and library and the marble floor of the main hall lies under linoleum (Wikipedia); it is a school and not open.',
  '',
  'Saint Ann\'s School, private: view only. '+ST,
  [W('Crescent Athletic Club House')],brick='no'),
N('brooklyn-general-post-office','Brooklyn General Post Office (Federal Building)','Brooklyn (Downtown)','nyc','civic','standing',40.69535,-73.98953,'exact','public','drive','','1885–1892',
  'Brooklyn\'s main post office and federal courthouse at 271–301 Cadman Plaza East. Mifflin E. Bell, supervising architect of the Treasury, began the Romanesque Revival design in 1885; his successor William A. Freret made it bolder, though some of Bell\'s detail, such as larger corner towers, was never built. Construction ended in 1891 and the building was occupied in 1892. A seven-storey addition in a matching style followed in 1933. It is a city landmark (1966) and on the National Register (1974), and now holds the Downtown Brooklyn post office station and the federal bankruptcy court.',
  'The four-storey original block: polished granite arches along the first storey, granite and terracotta walls, steep slate roofs with dormers and iron cresting, and the tower on the southwest corner. Inside (Wikipedia) the stairs and the 1892 courtrooms are lined with Tennessee marble. Post office lobby hours not checked.',
  '',
  'United States government (General Services Administration): a working post office and courthouse, with security at the court entrance. Look from Cadman Plaza East and the park opposite. Lobby hours not checked. Street parking is scarce downtown; no lot was looked for.',
  [W('Federal Building and Post Office (Brooklyn)')],brick='no'),
N('green-wood-cemetery-main-gate','Green-Wood Cemetery: the main gate','Brooklyn (Greenwood Heights)','nyc','burial','standing',40.65812,-73.99468,'exact','public','walk','cemeteries','1838',
  'Green-Wood was founded in 1838 as a rural cemetery, laid out by David Bates Douglass over glacial hills that include Battle Hill, Brooklyn\'s highest natural point, and holds about 570,000 burials on 478 acres. It was so popular that it has been called Brooklyn\'s first public park. Its main entrance at Fifth Avenue and 25th Street is a double-arched Gothic Revival gate by Richard Upjohn, built in 1861–1863. The cemetery is a National Historic Landmark and the gate a city landmark.',
  'The gate: Belleville, New Jersey brownstone, with panels of Nova Scotia limestone over the two gateways carved with New Testament scenes of death and resurrection. Beyond it, monuments and mausoleums in brownstone, marble, granite, bronze and cast zinc, some by Upjohn, Minard Lafever and Warren and Wetmore, and a chapel of 1913.',
  '',
  'The Green-Wood Cemetery, a working cemetery that welcomes visitors, free, 365 days a year (green-wood.com/our-hours, read November 2026). The gates are locked promptly at closing: be out ten minutes before. Vehicles and walkers may use the main entrance; the three other entrances keep shorter hours. Parking: the page lets vehicles in at the main gate, and OpenStreetMap maps parking just inside it; not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Green-Wood Cemetery'),L('Green-Wood: hours','https://www.green-wood.com/our-hours/')],th=[40.65781,-73.99521],thn='Parking mapped just inside the main gate (Landscape Avenue)',brick='no',
  hours='November 1, 2026 to March 14, 2027: main entrance 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. daily; longer in summer, see its page'),
]

